CONF68K known divergencesTest IDs listed here are disagreements with DOCS/claims.md that havealready been investigated and characterised. The runner reports them asKNOWN rather than as new findings, so that a familiar defect does notdrown out a fresh one. A line is "<test-id> <one-line explanation>".An empty list is the intended steady state: it means every claim inclaims.md holds on every system we can currently reach. It is no longerempty. Everything below was found by the record-locking tests on theirfirst run against an RBF image, and every one of them is os9exec's, notOS-9's -- they are recorded here so that a fix shows up as CHANGED, whichis news, instead of as silence.Note that all four are RBF-image findings. On a host-native directorythese tests report SKIP and prove nothing, which is why DOCS/expected andDOCS/expected-rbf are separate files.t19 A writer blocked on another process's record lock is never resumed,    even after the holder releases every lock it held, so the test reports    902 -- its own marker for "the child never came back" -- rather than    the 252 the manual specifies. Two separate faults produce this. First,    SS_Ticks is stored and then never consulted on the write path, so the    "return E$Lock rather than wait" contract the child asked for is not    honoured. Second, and worse, the write path never calls WokeOnFile, so    a process parked in pWaitRead by a write is not restored to its saved    state when the lock is released -- the read path does make that call    (file_rbf.c, "if (!wMode) WokeOnFile"), which is exactly why t21 passes    while this fails. t21 is the control that localises it: same lock, same    holder, contended by a read instead of a write, and it correctly    reports 252. Without the parent's unconditional rescue this test would    not fail, it would hang, and take the rest of the run with it.t23 A whole-file lock is measured from the current file pointer instead of    covering the file. SS_Lock is documented as "If $FFFFFFFF bytes are    requested, then the entire file is locked out regardless of where the    file pointer is"; os9exec sets the locked range to [current position,    $FFFFFFFF), so a holder that had seeked to 90 leaves bytes 0 to 89    unprotected and the contending write at 0 succeeds. The test seeks to    90 deliberately for this reason -- a holder and a contender working at    the same offset would not tell the two implementations apart.t25 EOF lock does not hold against another WRITER. The manual gives the    lock's purpose as avoiding "problems that could occur when two users    try to simultaneously extend a file", so a second process writing at    the end is the case it exists for; here that write succeeds. The lock    is not absent -- it is consulted on the read path, which is the half    that makes the documented spooler-behind-assembler behaviour work, so    the visible symptom is narrow: readers cannot pass the writer, and    writers can.t27 The single-user bit in an I$Open access mode is not enforced. I$Open    documents bit 6 as "open file for non sharable use" and page 7-8 says    a second open must return error 253; both opens succeed here. Unlike    the three above this involves no blocking at all, so it is the one    locking claim that would still report a real verdict on a system where    a blocked process is never resumed.t29 A process holding two paths to the same file is refused with E$DeadLk    (254) when it writes through one path into a record it locked through    the other. The manual makes this the recommended idiom, not an error:    "If an application requires more than one record to be locked out,    multiple paths to the same file may be opened with each path having its    own record locked out. RBF notices that the same process owns both paths    and keeps them from locking each other out." os9exec detects the same    ownership and draws the opposite conclusion -- file_rbf.c, "if    (spH->u.rbf.ownPid==currentpid) return os9error( E_DEADLK )". The check    was added to break a real self-deadlock between an update-mode follower    and a producer, which the surrounding comment documents; the manual's    answer to that situation is that the conflict should not arise at all.    Consequence for real programs: the documented multi-record locking    pattern returns spurious deadlock errors.